# Local multi-repository development.
#
# Rename to go.work to build against a local checkmate checkout:
#
#   cp go.work.example go.work
#
# This replaces the committed
# `replace github.com/adedayo/checkmate => ../checkmate` directive that used to
# live in go.mod. The difference matters. A replace in go.mod is part of the
# module and travels with every build, which is why the release pipeline had to
# strip it out and re-resolve the dependency to @main at build time. That made
# every release build depend on the state of another repository's default
# branch: a v2.1.0 tag did not identify the code that was built, two builds of
# the same tag were not the same artefact, and an SBOM or provenance statement
# over the result described a dependency graph that existed only during that
# CI run.
#
# go.work is git-ignored, local-only, and invisible to `go build` in CI. The
# committed go.mod now pins a released checkmate version, and the release
# pipeline builds the repository exactly as committed.
#
# Bumping the pin is a reviewed change, not a side effect of a rebuild:
#
#   go get github.com/adedayo/checkmate@v1.3.4

go 1.26.1

use (
.
../checkmate
)
